## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous network structures consisting of metal oxide clusters linked by molecular chains. This new class of materials shows promising applications in gas storage,1 luminescent detectors,2 molecular sieves,3 drug delivery,4 and catalysis.5 Meanwhile, many MOFs exhibit unique magnetic properties due to constitutive openshell transition metal ions, which has been attracting much attention6−10 because of their potential applications in low density magnetic biomedicine, molecular magnets, and magnetic molecular sensors. Understanding and chemically controlling the magnetic properties of these materials remains a key challenge. The magnetic properties of MOFs are mainly attributed to metal ions with unpaired electrons, and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}/$ ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ containing MOFs have been widely studied for their magnetic properties.1−3,6,9 Because of the strong superexchange coupling between two $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ions within a paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{(COO)}_{4},$ the secondary building unit containing a $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ dimer $\mathrm{'Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}S\mathrm{BU}s_{3}$ , Figure 1a) has antiferromagnetic (AFM) spin alignment with zero total magnetic moment.11 Thus, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ constructed MOFs are not expected to exhibit ferromagnetic (FM) features as observed for MOF-11 (1) with PtS topology.12 However, Williams et al. observed, for the first time, a FM feature in a three-dimensional (3D) $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SBU}$ constructed MOF, HKUST-1 (2), with a Curie temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{C}}\right)$ of $4.7~\mathrm{~K~}^{6}$ Later, Zaworotko et al. discovered structural dependent magnetic properties in $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{(m\mathbf{-}B D C)}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC}\ =\ 1,3{\cdot}\right.$ Benzenedicarboxylate) polymorphs.8 They found that triangularly arranged $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC})_{2}$ framework $\left(3,3\ \Delta\right)$ shows FM feature at $5\mathrm{\boldmath~K~},$ while at the same temperature, the ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{m}-1)$ $\mathrm{BDC})_{2}$ framework of square lattice $\left(4,4\boxed{}\right)$ does not. It is speculated that the different magnetic properties of the two aforementioned $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{m}{-}\mathbf{BDC})_{2}$ MOFs are due to the spin frustration in the triangular arrangement of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s.$ 8 Veciana et al. questioned such a hypothesis because a net spin is needed on each node to cause the spin frustration but the AFM $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SBU}$ does not possess a net spin.13 Theoretical calculations indicated that, in order to generate a net spin and subsequent spin frustration in an AFM $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SBU}$ , a minimal spin-flip energy of $630~\mathrm{\meV}$ is required [see Supporting Information, Figure S1]. Such a high energy barrier is impossible to overcome at $5~\mathrm{K}.$ Therefore, there remain two mysteries, how the strongly AFM coupled $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BUs}$ can generate a local magnetic moment and how these moments can be coupled over a large distance $({\sim}9.5\mathrm{\AA}$ in HKUST-1 which is the minimum separation of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BUs}^{\prime}$ ).
Motivated by vacancy induced magnetism in many materials such as graphene14 and dilute magnetic semiconductors (DMSs),15 we investigate the possibility of ferromagnetism induced by point defects, that is, Cu vacancy $(\mathrm{V_{Cu}}).$ , in a series of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SBU}$ based MOFs. The other defects, such as atomic C and O vacancies, are also calculated, but they cannot induce any magnetic moment due to the delocalization of their defect wave functions.15 Magnetic properties of five MOFs $\left(1{-}5\right)$ listed in Table 1 were investigated theoretically. Moreover, experimental studies were carried out on one of them, MOF-505 (5),16 in order to validate our theoretical predictions. On the basis of our density functional theory (DFT) calculations on these five MOFs and experimental observations on MOF (5), we conclude that the magnetic moments in these MOFs are originated from $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Cu}},$ which breaks the balance of the AFM state in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BUs}$ and creates quasi-localized spin states of $\mathsf{2p}$ orbital on oxygen (O) and carbon (C) atoms around it. These quasi-localized spin states are FM coupled via itinerant $\pi$ electrons in the conjugated aromatic linkers (Figure 1b).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
In ionic crystals of vacancy-induced magnetism, cationic vacancies favor the formation of local moments compared to anionic vacancies due to the localized cationic vacancy wave functions.15 At first, we consider a single $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ which is simulated by removing a $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ion from the supercell. Introducing the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ breaks the spin dimer. Taking MOF (5) as an example, our calculations predict that a single $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ favors a spin-polarized state with a spin-polarization energy of $199{\mathrm{~meV}}$ . Such strong spin polarization results in a complete separation of the majority and minority states and the formation of a local moment of $0.73~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ . We also calculate the C and $\mathrm{~o~}$ atomic vacancies in MOF (5) and find no formation of local magnetic moments, which is in good agreement with vacancy-induced magnetism in semiconductors.15 The localization of the magnetic moments of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ can also be visualized by plotting the defect state charge density in the real space. For example, Figure 2c,d shows the evidence that these defect states are strongly localized on the oxygen atoms surrounding the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ in MOF (5) and MOF (1), not on the another $\mathtt{C u}$ atom in the same dimer. This is because of the strong localization of copper defect wave functions.15 The formation energy of a single $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ can vary from $2.50~\mathrm{eV}$ under Cu-rich condition to $-0.44\ \mathrm{eV}$ under Cu-poor condition (see Supporting Information Figure S3), depending on the synthesis conditions. The negative formation energy under Cu-poor condition implies spontaneous formation of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ . Furthermore, vacancies are inevitable especially for large organic-metal complex systems such as MOFs during the chemical fabrication processes.16 It is difficult to obtain samples with well-defined vacancy concentrations as well as perfect crystal, but we still can provide direct evidence of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ in the synthesized MOF (5). We fabricated MOF (5) following the same method described in ref 16, and measured the extended $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectrum at the Cu K-edge. By comparing the spectrum of MOF (5) with a relatively good crystal $\mathbf{CuAc}_{2}$ sample (Figure
3a), we found that the $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ distance of the first shell is 1.95 ${\mathring{\mathrm{A}}},$ but the second $\mathrm{{Cu{-}C u}}$ ordering peak is absent. In contrast, a $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ ordering peak can be clearly seen in $\mathbf{CuAc}_{2}$ sample with a distance of 2.58 Å (Figure 3b). Both MOF (5) and $\mathbf{CuAc}_{2}$ samples possess similar $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ coordination in the second shell17 and the measurements were carried out under the same condition. The missing $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ peak in the EXAFS spectrum of MOF (5) is very likely due to the formation of Cu vacancies.
In addition, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to examine the composition of MOF (5), after the sample was annealed for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $400\mathrm{~K~}$ to remove possible adsorbed water molecules. On the basis of XPS spectrum in Figure 3c, we deduce a $\mathrm{{Cu/O}}$ ratio of 1:11.6 for our synthesized MOF (5), which is lower than that in MOF (5) with and without crystallized water, 1:10 and 1:7 respectively. This further suggests the existence of Cu vacancies. Moreover, based on the XPS results, the concentration of atomic Cu vacancies in the crystal surface of MOF (5) is estimated to be around $1.04\%$ . It is noted that the XPS spectrum only shows the surface chemical environment. Therefore, our calculated $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ concentration is not for bulk crystal. The bulk $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ concentration should be much lower than $1.04\%$ . Therefore, we also use Energydispersive $\mathrm{x}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) attached SEM system to evaluate the composition of this sample. It is found that the $\mathrm{{Cu/O}}$ ratio is approximately 1:9, which corresponds to a Cu vacancy of $0.57\%$ . Although we cannot provide very accurate $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ concentration in the synthesized MOF (5), we can confirm the existence of Cu vacancy in MOF (5) based on the three aforementioned analytical methods.
The spin densities of MOF (5) and MOF (1) with a single $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ each are shown in Figure 2, panels c and d, respectively. It is seen that $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ does generate defect spin states in both MOFs. These defects spin states show p-orbital characteristics and are mainly localized on oxygen and carbon atoms which are neighbors of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ . Remarkably, this is very similar to the cationic vacancy-induced FM in DMSs, which is due to the electron redistribution after removing cations.15 Our calculations indicate that $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ is responsible for the net spin states, and it is the origin of the local magnetic moments in these two MOFs. Similar results were obtained for other MOFs being studied.
As we know, the existence of local moments does not necessarily result in collective magnetism. Therefore, after identifying the origin of the local magnetic moments in MOFs, the next issue is whether the moments are coupled ferromagnetically or antiferromagnetically, long-range or short-range. A long-range ferromagnetic coupling is critical for achieving high temperature ferromagnetism at low defect concentrations, such as in MOFs. Different from those in dilute magnetic semiconductors (DMSs), defect states in MOFs are localized in regions separated by at least $14\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ and such a longrange FM coupling cannot be mediated by simple superexchange interaction involving either $\mathsf{2p}$ orbitals of O or C atoms, or 3d orbital of Cu ions because of their localized special distribution of wave functions (see Supporting Information, Figure S4).15 Furthermore, the common types of ferromagnetic mechanism are excluded in $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ -MOFs (see Supporting Information Section IV and Figure S4). Recently, it was proposed that the delocalized $\pi$ electrons in graphene are able to mediate spin coupling over a long-range in the defectinduced “Stoner” magnetism, where the coupling distance between the local moments induced by carbon vacancies can reach as large as $20\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in graphene through the RudermanKittel-Kasuya-Yosida (RKKY)-like interaction.14,18 In MOF (5), the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BUs}$ are linked by biphenyl (BP) which has conjugated $\pi$ electrons similar to that in graphene. It is thus reasonable to speculate that the long-range FM coupling between the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ induced defect spin states could be mediated by these delocalized itinerant $\pi$ electrons in the BP linkers.
To verify such a hypothesis, we calculated the magnetic property of two copper vacancies located in neighboring $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}-$ SBUs which are modeled by removing one $\dot{\mathrm{Cu(II)}}$ ion from each of the two neighboring $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ in the aforementioned MOFs. We note that due to the unique structure of MOFs, FM coupling can be established at very low defect concentration. Taking again MOF (5) as an example, each $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ building unit has 12 neighboring $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ building units directly connected by the BP linkers. Theoretically, the minimum concentration of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ required to have two copper vacancies directly connected by one BP linker in MOF (5) is much less than $1\%$ (see Supporting Information, Figure S5). This implies that the copper vacancies can be in the coupling range even at very low concentration, which is in good agreement with our above experiment. As a matter of fact, it has been reported that a defect concentration of $1\%$ is sufficient to generate detectable long-range FM ordering in diluted magnetic semiconductors at room temperature.15
The calculated results for all five MOFs being studied, including energy difference $(\Delta E)$ between the parallel and the antiparallel spin configurations, the distance between two neighboring $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Cu}},$ and the induced magnetic moment are given in Table 1. Available experimental Curie temperatures $\left(T_{\mathrm{C}}\right)$ are also listed in the table. We compare the results of MOF (1) and MOF (5) first. The two $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ in these two MOFs have similar separations, that is, $14.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in MOF (1) and $14.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in MOF (5) (Figure 2e,f). The linker in MOF (5) is BP, an aromatic organic chain with delocalized $\pi$ electrons (Figure 1b) but in MOF (1) is adamantine (ATC) that does not have delocalized electrons (Figure 1c). On the basis of the above hypothesis, FM coupling is expected in MOF (5) but not in MOF (1).16
In addition, the results of our calculations have confirmed that MOF (5) favors FM coupling, with a magnetic moment of $1.71\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ per pair of copper vacancies, and the energy of the FM state is $59\mathrm{meV}$ lower than that of the AFM state (Table 1). In contrast, the FM and AFM states of MOF (1) have comparable energies, suggesting weak magnetic interaction between the two copper vacancies (Figure 2f). These results are consistent with the experimental observation.6 It is noted that the aromatic linkers have graphene-like two sublattices and the spin states in MOF (5) are localized on the same sublattice of the aromatic linkers only, as shown in Figure 2e. This results in ferromagnetic coupling without a RKKY-like oscillating behavior.
The proposed mechanism also provides an explanation on why MOFs having the same building block can show completely different magnetic properties. It is the itinerant electrons in the linker that play a key role in the long-range magnetic coupling. Besides MOF (5) and MOF (1), our calculations suggest that the remaining three MOFs $(2{-}4)$ all show FM coupling (Table 1). The only difference of the ferromagnetic behavior between them is their different Curie temperature. This is because the linkers in these structures (TMA and $\mathbf{m}$ -BDC) are also conjugated chains with delocalized $\pi$ electrons, similar to the BP linkers in MOF (5). Of the four FM MOFs, MOF (4) has the smallest energy difference between the FM and AFM states ( $\cdot\Delta E=-32\mathrm{\meV},$ ). Compared to other FM MOFs, a lower $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ is expected for MOF (4) due to the weaker FM coupling between the copper vacancies. In comparison, MOF (2) shows a large energy difference ( $\Delta E=$ $-40\ \mathrm{\meV})$ and a much shorter vacancy separation (9.5 Å). Since the $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ of MOF (2) is $4.7\mathrm{K},$ MOF (4) is expected to have a ferromagnetic ordering temperature lower than $4.7~\mathrm{K},$ which could be the reason why MOF (4)’s FM feature is not observed at $5~\mathrm{K}^{8}$ The distance between two Cu vacancies is related to the concentration of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ if the doping is uniform in experiment. Therefore, the calculated reference values of concentration of $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ are also listed in Table 1.
To provide further support to the proposed mechanism, we measured the magnetic-hysteresis (M-H) loops for MOF (5) at 5 and $20~\mathrm{K},$ respectively, and show the results in Figure 4a. A well-defined M-H loop can be seen at $5~\mathrm{\K},$ indicating ferromagnetic ordering in the sample. Furthermore, we measured the temperature dependency susceptibility to obtain the Curie temperature. The result is shown in Figure 4b, where the inset shows the inverse susceptibility. By fitting the data to the Curie−Weiss law, a $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ of $11\mathrm{~K~}$ is obtained for MOF (5), which is higher than that of MOF (2) (4.7 K), as expected. It is worth pointing out that the organic molecular linker in MOF (5) is different from a usual biphenyl group. Due to the constraint in the network structure, the biphenyl group in MOF (5) has a planar structure which is an excellent conjugated system providing effective mediation of ferromagnetism originated from $\mathrm{V_{Cu}}$ . This unique structural advantage could be the reason for the higher Curie temperature of MOF (5) compared to that of substituted benzenecarboxylate conjugated systems such as that in MOF (2).
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, based on the DFT calculated magnetic properties of a series of MOFs $\left(1-5\right)$ and experimental investigations on MOF (5), we elucidated why some AFM $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ constructed MOFs exhibit FM features, and identified Cu vacancies as the origin of localized spin states and local moments. The collective long-range ferromagnetism is due to the coupling between these localized spin states via delocalized $\pi$ electrons in conjugated aromatic linkers. The copper defect states and itinerant $\pi$ electrons in the linkers play the key role in long-range ferromagnetism in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ based MOFs. The proposed mechanism is confirmed by direct experimental evidence of copper vacancies and long-range collective ferromagnetism in MOF (5). This mechanism is expected to be general and can hold in a diverse range of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ based MOFs.
In addition, our work demonstrated that it is possible to make use of the rich organic chemistry to construct highly conjugated aromatic linkers that provide magnetic coupling between the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{C u}}$ induced magnetic moments to obtain ferromagnetism. In fact, organic linkers containing aromatic units have been recently assembled into highly porous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{-}$ SBUs based MOFs.19 Our work has highlighted the bright promise of such $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{-}S\mathrm{BU}s$ based MOFs as excellent novel FM porous materials. The understanding of the long-range ferromagnetic ordering materials is valuable in further exploration of this wide class of materials and in designing new high $T_{\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SBUs}$ based FM MOFs.
